    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to determine the range of values that \\( f(x) \\) can take. The function \\( f(x) \\) is a quadratic function, and its graph is a parabola that opens upwards (since the coefficient of \\( x^2 \\) is positive).\n\nThe vertex of the parabola \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\) gives the minimum value of the function. The x-coordinate of the vertex of a quadratic function \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\) is given by \\( x = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\). Here, \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 3 \\), so the x-coordinate of the vertex is:\n\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{3}{2 \\cdot 1} = -\\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nTo find the y-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute \\( x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\) into the function \\( f(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nf\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) = \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2 + 3\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) + c = \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{9}{2} + c = \\frac{9}{4} - \\frac{18}{4} + c = -\\frac{9}{4} + c\n\\]\n\nSince the parabola opens upwards, the minimum value of \\( f(x) \\) is \\( -\\frac{9}{4} + c \\). For \\(-2\\) to be in the range of \\( f(x) \\), we need:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{9}{4} + c \\leq -2\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\( c \\), we add \\( \\frac{9}{4} \\) to both sides of the inequality:\n\n\\[\nc \\leq -2 + \\frac{9}{4} = -\\frac{8}{4} + \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of \\( f(x) \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
